  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "⛰️ Goliath Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Birth name, nickname and clan name: goliath names in the three parts D&D gives them."
    },
    "intro": "Goliaths are the mountain-dwelling, giant-blooded people of Dungeons &amp; Dragons, and each one carries three names: one from their parents, one from their chief and one from their clan. This **goliath name generator** builds all three together, or any part on its own. It's a fan-made, unofficial page: D&amp;D and its goliaths belong to Wizards of the Coast, and every name here is new.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good goliath names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator, across every format:</p><ul><li><strong>Haragun \"Stonethrower\" Vela-Anaki</strong>: a full name with a hyphenated clan, and a nickname that tells you he won something by throwing it.</li><li><strong>Arilani \"Stepcarver\" Ravanekanua</strong>: the goliath who cut the steps up the cliff, so the whole clan uses her name every time they climb.</li><li><strong>Thorakan</strong>: a male birth name, short and hard, that shouts well across a valley.</li><li><strong>Ikamei</strong>: a female birth name with the open vowel ending goliath names often have.</li><li><strong>Twice-Lucky</strong>: a nickname with a story in it. Two falls, two survivals; nobody wants to see a third.</li><li><strong>Mule-Talker</strong>: a fond, slightly teasing nickname for the one the pack animals listen to.</li><li><strong>Hailcatcher</strong>: short, odd and memorable, which is what a good nickname should be.</li><li><strong>Hulokathimathi</strong>: a clan name in the long, rolling style, ending in a vowel.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "How goliath names work",
        "content": "<p>The fifth-edition goliath writeups, first in the <em>Elemental Evil Player's Companion</em> (2015) and then in <em>Volo's Guide to Monsters</em> (2016), give every goliath three names:</p><ul><li><strong>A birth name</strong>, chosen by the parents. It's short, up to three syllables, and the books don't split birth names into male and female.</li><li><strong>A nickname</strong>, given by the tribal chief. It describes the goliath, and it can change during their life as they do new things.</li><li><strong>A clan name</strong>, which is much longer and ends in a vowel.</li></ul><p>Written out, the nickname sits in the middle, usually in quotation marks: Thorakan \"Hailcatcher\" Hulokathimathi. Because birth names aren't gendered in the books, the Male and Female choices here go by sound only: male names end on hard sounds like <em>-vak</em> and <em>-ruk</em>, female names on a vowel. Use Any if you'd rather not choose.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "What a nickname says about your goliath",
        "content": "<p>Goliaths are described as fiercely competitive: they keep score of their own deeds and expect a fair contest. The nickname is where that shows. It's the name other people actually use, and it's a running record of what the goliath did last.</p><ul><li><strong>Deed names</strong> like Stonethrower, Cragclimber or Elkcaller mark one moment the tribe remembers.</li><li><strong>Habit names</strong> like Late-Riser, Goat-Counter or Slow-to-Anger come from living alongside someone for years.</li><li><strong>Character names</strong> like Unbowed, Level-Headed or Soft-Spoken are what the chief thinks of them.</li></ul><p>Since the chief can change it, the nickname is also a ready-made story arc. Choose the nickname your goliath has now, then choose the one they want to earn, and play towards it.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Giant ancestry and sound",
        "content": "<p>In the 2024 <em>Player's Handbook</em>, goliaths choose a giant ancestry (cloud, fire, frost, hill, stone or storm), which gives them a boon such as Frost's Chill or Stone's Endurance. It doesn't change how the name is built, but it's a good guide when you pick a nickname: a frost-blooded goliath suits Hailcatcher or Glacierwalker, a storm-blooded one Thundersinger.</p><p>For the full-sized giants themselves, with names for each kind, try the <a href=\"giant-name-generator.html\">Giant Name Generator</a>. For another D&amp;D people with a clan-first naming rule, see the <a href=\"dragonborn-name-generator.html\">Dragonborn Name Generator</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "How do goliath names work in D&D?",
        "answer": "A goliath has a short birth name from their parents, a nickname from their chief and a long clan name, in that order. Choose Full name (three parts) to get all three, like Haragun \"Stonethrower\" Vela-Anaki."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are some good female goliath names?",
        "answer": "Choose Female and Birth name only for names like Ikamei, Arilani or Vekalani. The books don't split goliath birth names by gender, so any birth name here works for any goliath."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is a goliath nickname?",
        "answer": "It's the name a goliath's chief gives them, describing a deed, a habit or their character, and it can change over their life. Choose Nickname only for ones like Twice-Lucky, Mule-Talker or Peakwatcher."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are good goliath clan names?",
        "answer": "Long names that end in a vowel, like Hulokathimathi or Ravanekanua, or hyphenated ones like Vela-Anaki. Choose Clan name only for more."
      },
      {
        "question": "Are these names from the Player's Handbook?",
        "answer": "No. They're new names in the same style, and the example names from the D&amp;D books are left out, so your goliath won't share a name with one from a sourcebook."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I make a goliath name that starts with a certain letter?",
        "answer": "Yes. Use Starts with to choose the letter; it works on the birth name for full names, and on the nickname or clan name when you choose those formats."
      }
    ]
  }
